WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:01.795
MARK MIRCHANDANI:
Cloud migration

00:00:01.795 --> 00:00:04.250
is top of mind for many
organizations today.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:06.480
While moving to the cloud
can be full of challenges,

00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.570
the cloud offers many advantages
around increased agility,

00:00:09.570 --> 00:00:12.600
new and innovative services,
and on-demand pricing

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.950
that traditional data
centers don't offer.

00:00:14.950 --> 00:00:18.120
So let's talk about one way
to make migration easier--

00:00:18.120 --> 00:00:20.520
hosting your native
VMware environment right

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:22.410
on Google Cloud.

00:00:22.410 --> 00:00:24.780
The reality is that managing
your own infrastructure

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:27.780
is often an unnecessary
burden and usually

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:30.360
isn't a differentiator
for the business.

00:00:30.360 --> 00:00:32.400
While migrating to the
cloud is the end goal,

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.770
there can be significant
complexity in refactoring

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.470
applications, as well as
the tools and policies

00:00:37.470 --> 00:00:38.910
your teams use.

00:00:38.910 --> 00:00:41.250
The challenge of migration
can involve risks

00:00:41.250 --> 00:00:44.160
and put your business
in analysis paralysis.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.170
Google Cloud wants to
help businesses capture

00:00:46.170 --> 00:00:48.690
the benefits of cloud while
reducing the complexity

00:00:48.690 --> 00:00:50.285
of doing a full refactor.

00:00:50.285 --> 00:00:51.660
Today we're going
to show you how

00:00:51.660 --> 00:00:53.760
that's possible with
Google Cloud VMware

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:56.340
Engine, a fast and safe
way to move your VMware

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:58.240
workloads to the cloud.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:00.083
Google Cloud VMware
Engine is built

00:01:00.083 --> 00:01:01.500
to address the
biggest issues that

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.840
prevent most workloads
from moving to the cloud--

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:07.620
lack of resources and the
cost of re-architecting apps.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:10.350
With Google Cloud VMware Engine,
you can migrate your apps

00:01:10.350 --> 00:01:11.730
with zero changes.

00:01:11.730 --> 00:01:14.820
You'll be able to run your
VMs in a private native VMware

00:01:14.820 --> 00:01:15.910
environment.

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.208
This means you can use the same
tools, processes, and policies

00:01:19.208 --> 00:01:21.000
while still getting
the advantages of being

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:23.730
in the cloud, on top of deeper
integration with other Google

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:25.170
Cloud services.

00:01:25.170 --> 00:01:27.830
In fact, it doesn't take long
to spin up an environment.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:29.580
You can lower your
total cost of ownership

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:31.800
quickly and spend
more time planning how

00:01:31.800 --> 00:01:33.483
to re-architect down the road.

00:01:33.483 --> 00:01:36.150
Let's walk through a demo of how
to set up a Google Cloud VMware

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:38.570
environment with
just a few clicks.

00:01:38.570 --> 00:01:41.070
We'll start by showing how to
access the Google Cloud VMware

00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.230
Engine from the
Google Cloud console.

00:01:43.230 --> 00:01:45.900
After that, we'll create a
secure and isolated boundary

00:01:45.900 --> 00:01:48.858
called a private cloud that will
contain the VMware environment.

00:01:48.858 --> 00:01:51.150
Then we'll take a tour of
some of the platform features

00:01:51.150 --> 00:01:53.418
and jump into the familiar
vSphere interface.

00:01:53.418 --> 00:01:55.710
Let's start with the icon in
the upper left-hand corner

00:01:55.710 --> 00:01:56.592
of the console.

00:01:56.592 --> 00:01:58.050
Scroll down to the
Compute section,

00:01:58.050 --> 00:01:59.700
and you'll see VMware Engine.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:01.410
Clicking on this takes
us to the overview

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.420
page, which provides
additional details

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:04.890
and lets us do
common operations,

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:08.370
like creating our private
cloud, viewing documentation,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.400
launching the vSphere client,
modifying network settings,

00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:13.107
and managing users.

00:02:13.107 --> 00:02:15.690
Let's create a private cloud by
clicking on the Create Private

00:02:15.690 --> 00:02:16.982
Cloud button.

00:02:16.982 --> 00:02:18.690
Here we'll be able to
customize our cloud

00:02:18.690 --> 00:02:20.203
by choosing a location.

00:02:20.203 --> 00:02:22.620
Let's leave it on US east and
increase the number of nodes

00:02:22.620 --> 00:02:23.850
from 3 to 4.

00:02:23.850 --> 00:02:26.340
We'll also add a CIDR
range for our appliances

00:02:26.340 --> 00:02:28.558
and then click
Review and Create.

00:02:28.558 --> 00:02:30.350
After reviewing that
everything looks good,

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:33.060
we'll click Create to
kick off the process.

00:02:33.060 --> 00:02:35.670
Once completed, we can see
the details of the environment

00:02:35.670 --> 00:02:38.622
as well as other private
clouds that we've created.

00:02:38.622 --> 00:02:40.330
While the private
cloud is being created,

00:02:40.330 --> 00:02:41.850
let's talk about pricing.

00:02:41.850 --> 00:02:44.950
Pricing is done per node and
includes all the storage,

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:48.400
compute, and licensing to
run a VMware environment.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.230
By default, you'll
pay per month,

00:02:50.230 --> 00:02:52.950
but you can also sign up
for a one or three-year plan

00:02:52.950 --> 00:02:54.330
to save money.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:56.070
All of the billing
for VMware Engine

00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:58.080
is integrated so you
can see payment details

00:02:58.080 --> 00:03:01.530
side by side with your
other Google Cloud services.

00:03:01.530 --> 00:03:03.250
After your private
cloud is created,

00:03:03.250 --> 00:03:05.130
you can access it from
the Resources screen.

00:03:05.130 --> 00:03:08.280
Here you can see all of your
private clouds and manage them.

00:03:08.280 --> 00:03:10.180
By clicking on a
specific private cloud,

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.480
you can see the details
of its configuration.

00:03:12.480 --> 00:03:14.440
Here you can launch your
VirtualCenter client,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:16.940
which is the standard enterprise
VirtualCenter client you're

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:18.270
likely already familiar with.

00:03:18.270 --> 00:03:20.917
You can also expand the cloud
by adding nodes, as well

00:03:20.917 --> 00:03:22.500
as scrolling down
to the bottom, where

00:03:22.500 --> 00:03:25.410
you can shrink your environment,
escalate your privileges so you

00:03:25.410 --> 00:03:27.480
can get admin access
in VirtualCenter,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.745
or even delete the
private cloud entirely.

00:03:29.745 --> 00:03:31.620
Let's scroll back up to
the top of the screen

00:03:31.620 --> 00:03:33.300
and launch the vSphere client.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:35.910
On this login page, we
can enter our credentials.

00:03:35.910 --> 00:03:37.650
After logging in, you
can see that we're

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:39.900
working with the same interface
that you and your team

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:41.550
are likely already
familiar with.

00:03:41.550 --> 00:03:44.280
This native access to VMware
gives you a standard way

00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:47.010
to control your applications
while getting all the benefits

00:03:47.010 --> 00:03:49.140
of running on Google Cloud.

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:51.720
Now let's return to the Google
Cloud VMware Engine management

00:03:51.720 --> 00:03:54.900
console and look at some
of the networking details.

00:03:54.900 --> 00:03:58.410
Here we can manage and set up
our VLANs and subnets, firewall

00:03:58.410 --> 00:04:04.200
tables, public IP addresses,
VPN gateways, and VPC Peerings,

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.170
where you can easily access
your Google Cloud infrastructure

00:04:07.170 --> 00:04:08.870
from your VMware environment.

00:04:08.870 --> 00:04:10.595
The activities interface
provides details

00:04:10.595 --> 00:04:11.970
that your security
and operations

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:13.590
teams will be interested in.

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:16.230
It provides any alerts that have
occurred in the environment,

00:04:16.230 --> 00:04:18.720
details on any events that
have occurred in the past,

00:04:18.720 --> 00:04:21.149
current running
tasks, and allows

00:04:21.149 --> 00:04:24.030
you to audit any
activities done by a user.

00:04:24.030 --> 00:04:27.120
The Account screen gives you an
overview of your entire VMware

00:04:27.120 --> 00:04:29.100
Engine environment,
including the ability

00:04:29.100 --> 00:04:30.990
to sign up for email alerts.

00:04:30.990 --> 00:04:32.700
You're also able to
manage the users who

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:34.590
have access to the environment.

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.470
We're eager for everyone to
see Google Cloud VMware Engine

00:04:37.470 --> 00:04:39.660
and are excited to say
that it's available for you

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:41.232
to try out right now.

00:04:41.232 --> 00:04:42.690
With this quick
tour, you should be

00:04:42.690 --> 00:04:45.480
ready to migrate your VMware
environment to the cloud

00:04:45.480 --> 00:04:46.310
quickly.

00:04:46.310 --> 00:04:48.600
Check out the documentation
and see more details

00:04:48.600 --> 00:04:50.280
and spin up your
own private cloud.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.630
[MUSIC PLAYING]

